      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "<!-- greptile_summary -->\n\n<h2><a href=\"https://app.greptile.com/api/retrigger?id=76887552\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/RetriggerDark.svg?v=2\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/Retrigger.svg?v=2\"><img alt=\"Retrigger\" src=\"https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/Retrigger.svg?v=2\" align=\"right\"></picture></a>Confidence Score: 5/5</h2>\n\n**[Medium risk]** Build configuration for C\\+\\+ distributed tests with optional Python linking\\.\n\nThis PR appears safe to merge; both distributed test executables receive the required Python linkage when CuTeDSL is enabled.\n\nWhat we checked:\n- Both tests link Python: The file defines two executables, and the added loop links Python to both.\n\n<h3>Summary</h3>\n\nAdds Python linkage to the distributed C++ tests when `NVTE_WITH_CUTEDSL` is enabled.\n- Uses the same environment-based option and linking approach as the existing C++ tests.\n- Covers both `test_comm_gemm` and `test_ep`.\n- No actionable issues were found.\n\n<!-- greptile_confidence_score:5 -->\n\n<sub>Reviews (1) · Last reviewed commit: [\"\\[Common\\] Link python to C++ dist tests w...\"](https://github.com/nvidia/transformerengine/commit/00cb0378209c748776cd04a6d4d4ebe311851c17) · [Reviewed by Greptile](https://www.greptile.com/?utm_source=greptile_expert&utm_medium=github&utm_campaign=code_reviews&utm_content=nvidia/transformerengine)</sub>",
